      "paperId": "6a0684a087f997b0400be1d1eef95bbc10fb2932",
      "title": "The origin of Russian absolutism in the context of the military revolution theory",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "S. Nefedov",
          "authorId": "37575754"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2022,
      "abstract": "The article is devoted to the interpretation of socio-political processes during the reign of Ivan the Terrible from the standpoint of the theory of military revolution. Following the well-known work of B. Downing, the author identifies ten stages in the evolution of the medieval monarchy to militarybureaucratic absolutism. Such cases are described in the history of the Scandinavian states. Further, the socio-political processes of the times of Ivan the Terrible are analyzed from the point of view of their correspondence to the distinguished steps. It is concluded that these processes follow the scheme of B. Downing. The starting point of development is the medieval monarchy, in which the power of the suzerain is limited to assemblies (conditionally, \u00abparliaments\u00bb), where aristocrats predominate, managing the resources of their land holdings. The military revolution downgrades the importance of chivalry and requires the creation of a large army of firearms-wielding mercenary infantrymen. Funding a new army requires a reallocation of resources to the detriment of the aristocracy. The \u00abParliaments\u00bb resist these demands, and a conflict ensues between the monarchs and the aristocracy. The new army ensures the victory of the monarchs. Monarchs abolish \u00abparliaments\u00bb or deprive them of real powers. The victorious monarchs subdue the nobility, take away some of its resources. Reforms are being carried out, a new financial system is being created. A new bureaucracy is being created, and commoners are recruited for positions. The nobility is trying to fit into these new structures by taking office positions. Taking into account the criticism of the concept of \u00ababsolutism\u00bb in the context of the possibility of informal compromises between the monarch and the aristocracy, the author confines himself to considering cases where such compromises obviously could not have happened, when the monarch completely subordinated the aristocracy. The author notes that this conclusion is preliminary, and the problem requires further detailed research.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.21638/spbu19.2022.201",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6a0684a087f997b0400be1d1eef95bbc10fb2932",
      "venue": "Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana",
      "journal": {
        "name": "Studia Slavica et Balcanica Petropolitana"
      },
      "publicationTypes": [
        "JournalArticle"
      ]
    },
    {
      "paperId": "8669844547e24e22859af11275d7ec2ba278ecc4",
      "title": "Two-stage Robust optimization Method for Day-ahead Scheduling Considering Renewable Portfolio Standard",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Lupeng Qiao",
          "authorId": "1500412912"
        },
        {
          "name": "Ling Dong",
          "authorId": "49536441"
        },
        {
          "name": "Tianwen Zheng",
          "authorId": "30679651"
        },
        {
          "name": "Laijun Chen",
          "authorId": "2429690"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "Renewable Portfolio Standard (RPS) can greatly promote clean energy consumption and energy revolution. However, the randomness and volatility of renewable energy output pose challenges to fulfilling the RPS obligation. In order to meet the challenge, a day-ahead scheduling model considering the policy of RPS based on the two-stage robust framework is established in this paper. In this model, the inter-regional transmission constraints of renewable energy are considered because of the extremely uneven geographical distribution of renewable energy in China. In algorithm ways, the model is solved with a column-and-constraint generation method. The master problem is linearized to a mixed integer programming and the sub-problem is converted by strong duality theory rather than KKT equations. The result of case study demonstrates that with the rise of quota ratio and uncertainty budget, the uncertainty becomes increasingly unfavorable to the fulfillment of the policy of RPS. In addition, the superiority of the two-stage robust approach is illustrated.",
      "citationCount": 1,
      "doi": "10.1109/APPEEC45492.2019.8994687",
      "arxivId": null,
      "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8669844547e24e22859af11275d7ec2ba278ecc4",
      "venue": "IEEE PES Asia-Pacific Power and Energy Engineering Conference",
      "journal": {
        "name": "2019 IEEE PES Asia-Pacific Power and Energy Engineering Conference (APPEEC)",
        "pages": "1-5"
      },
      "publicationTypes": [
        "Conference"
      ]
    }
